since: 21

id: time-zones/extrapolation-offset/0505
area: time-zones
op: GetOffsetNanosecondsFor
in: str:"America/New_York" bigint:8640000000000000000000
out: value: f64:0xC2AA3185C5000000
class: divergence
divergence.broiler-js: row Z4: Broiler.JS reads only the TZif data block and keeps the offset of the last transition of 2037 for every later instant, so northern zones stay on standard time and southern ones on daylight time, where SJL-D-20 continues the recurring rules without limit; policy difference under SJL-D-20 (extrapolation), answered by SJL-8.6
prov: spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-time-zone-identifiers spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-getnamedtimezoneoffsetnanoseconds ref:tzref@1
script: Temporal.Instant.fromEpochNanoseconds(8640000000000000000000n).toZonedDateTimeISO("America/New_York").offsetNanoseconds
note: row Z4's cell; at +275760-09-13T00:00:00Z
since: 21

id: time-zones/extrapolation-wall/0001
area: time-zones
op: GetPossibleEpochNanoseconds
in: str:"America/New_York" int:2040 int:3 int:11 int:2 int:30 int:0
out: value: str:""
class: divergence
divergence.broiler-js: row A5: after 2037 Broiler.JS's frozen offset (row Z4) has no gap and no overlap, so a skipped or repeated wall time names one instant and reject accepts it; policy difference under SJL-D-20 (extrapolation), answered by SJL-8.6
prov: spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-time-zone-identifiers spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-getnamedtimezoneepochnanoseconds ref:tzref@1
script: var w = new Temporal.PlainDateTime(2040, 3, 11, 2, 30, 0); var r = []; ["earlier", "later"].forEach(function (m) { var z = w.toZonedDateTime("America/New_York", { disambiguation: m }); var n = String(z.epochNanoseconds); if (z.toPlainDateTime().equals(w) && r.indexOf(n) < 0) r.push(n); }); r.join()
note: row A5, a wall time after 2037; the instants the wall time names, through the two that earlier and later pick (at most two in the data)
since: 21

