out: value: f64:0x4020000000000000
class: normative
prov: spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-time-zone-identifiers spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-getnamedtimezoneoffsetnanoseconds ref:tzref@1
script: var p = new Intl.DateTimeFormat("en", { timeZone: "America/New_York", hour: "numeric", hourCycle: "h23", numberingSystem: "latn" }).formatToParts(new Date(2130062400000)); var h = null; for (var i = 0; i < p.length; i++) if (p[i].type === "hour") h = parseInt(p[i].value, 10); h
note: row Z4, the formatter's zone; the hour of 2037-07-01T12:00:00Z on a 23-hour clock in Latin digits, its padding aside
since: 21

id: time-zones/extrapolation-format/0002
area: time-zones
op: Intl.DateTimeFormat, hour
in: str:"America/New_York" int:2224756800000
out: value: f64:0x4020000000000000
class: divergence
divergence.broiler-js: row Z4: Broiler.JS reads only the TZif data block and keeps the offset of the last transition of 2037 for every later instant, so northern zones stay on standard time and southern ones on daylight time, where SJL-D-20 continues the recurring rules without limit; policy difference under SJL-D-20 (extrapolation), answered by SJL-8.6
prov: spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-time-zone-identifiers spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-getnamedtimezoneoffsetnanoseconds ref:tzref@1
script: var p = new Intl.DateTimeFormat("en", { timeZone: "America/New_York", hour: "numeric", hourCycle: "h23", numberingSystem: "latn" }).formatToParts(new Date(2224756800000)); var h = null; for (var i = 0; i < p.length; i++) if (p[i].type === "hour") h = parseInt(p[i].value, 10); h
note: row Z4, the formatter's zone; the hour of 2040-07-01T12:00:00Z on a 23-hour clock in Latin digits, its padding aside
since: 21

